WEBVTT

00:04.000 --> 00:08.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Openness, research, and privacy. Can we have

00:08.000 --> 00:12.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
it all? Every month, Wikipedia is visited

00:12.000 --> 00:16.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
over 15 billion times. But it's not just a go-to destination for

00:16.000 --> 00:20.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
curious readers. Wikipedia is also a vital resource for

00:20.000 --> 00:24.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
researchers around the world. Did you know that on average every year researchers

00:24.000 --> 00:28.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
use or refer to Wikipedia and more than 130,000

00:28.000 --> 00:32.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
articles and publish a minimum of roughly 500 research papers about

00:32.000 --> 00:36.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Wikipedia itself. But there's a side of this research that's less

00:36.000 --> 00:40.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
discussed. How it affects the people behind the

00:40.000 --> 00:44.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
edits. Wikipedia may be open to all, but its openness introduces

00:44.000 --> 00:48.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
a tension between transparency and privacy. Every

00:48.000 --> 00:52.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
edit is public. Every talk page is searchable. It's an

00:52.000 --> 00:56.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
invaluable resource for researchers who have a commitment to use their skills to

00:56.000 --> 01:00.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
transparently communicate to the world the opportunities they

01:00.000 --> 01:04.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
see. Meanwhile, editors come from all over the world as well.

01:04.000 --> 01:08.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Some use their real names, others work anonymously, sometimes because their

01:08.000 --> 01:12.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
edits could put them at risk, especially in places with censorship or

01:12.000 --> 01:16.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
political unrest. It can be difficult for researchers to balance the

01:16.000 --> 01:20.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
obligation for transparent research communication with privacy

01:20.000 --> 01:24.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
and anonymity expectations.

01:24.000 --> 01:28.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
In response to a request from the English Wikipedia Arbitration Committee,

01:28.000 --> 01:32.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
the Wikipdia Foundation prioritized a white paper. Its goal is to

01:32.000 --> 01:36.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
offer guidance to researchers on how to study Wikipedia in

01:36.000 --> 01:40.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
ways that respect editor privacy and community

01:40.000 --> 01:44.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
values. In collaboration between the Wikipedia Foundation's research

01:44.000 --> 01:48.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
team, human rights team, and an external research ethicist,

01:48.000 --> 01:52.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
the research and privacy on Wikipedia white paper was recently

01:52.000 --> 01:56.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
published as an open science framework preprint. Today, I want to give you

01:56.000 --> 02:00.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
a preview of what you can find in this white paper.

02:00.000 --> 02:04.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
We provide recommendations to researchers. For one, for example, we

02:04.000 --> 02:08.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
ask researchers to know the norms and guidelines, review Wikipedia's

02:08.000 --> 02:12.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
terms of use and the universal code of conduct before they start

02:12.000 --> 02:16.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
collecting or analyzing data. Create a public

02:16.000 --> 02:20.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
project page on metawiki. Avoid

02:20.000 --> 02:24.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
publishing usernames, diffs, or generally sharing information if it could be used to

02:24.000 --> 02:28.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
identify contributors, especially without

02:28.000 --> 02:32.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
consent. What's low risk in one country might be dangerous in

02:32.000 --> 02:36.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
another. Consider editor's geographic, political, and social

02:36.000 --> 02:40.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
contexts. And finally, recognize the significance of

02:40.000 --> 02:44.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
trade-offs you may have to make when balancing your obligations as a

02:44.000 --> 02:48.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
researcher to navigate the tension between sharing

02:48.000 --> 02:52.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
transparently while also maintaining the privacy and anonymity of readers and

02:52.000 --> 02:56.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
editors. The paper also

02:56.000 --> 03:00.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
contains tips and guidance for

03:00.000 --> 03:04.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Wikipedians. First, learn about trade-offs researchers need to

03:04.000 --> 03:08.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
make. One of the challenges researchers face when studying Wikipedia and

03:08.000 --> 03:12.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
communicating their findings is balancing their obligation to share the

03:12.000 --> 03:16.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
truth with the world in a transparent way while respecting

03:16.000 --> 03:20.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Wikipedia community's diverse and varying expectations with

03:20.000 --> 03:24.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
regards to privacy. As a result, sometimes the

03:24.000 --> 03:28.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
choices researchers may make in tension with what as a

03:28.000 --> 03:32.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Wikipedian you would expect or wish for. In the white

03:32.000 --> 03:36.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
paper, we provide some guidance about how to plan for and take action in such

03:36.000 --> 03:40.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
cases. Understand the risks.

03:40.000 --> 03:44.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Even your editing history can reveal clues about your identity. Be

03:44.000 --> 03:48.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
especially cautious if you edit on politically, culturally, socially, or

03:48.000 --> 03:52.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
religiously sensitive topics. Explore the

03:52.000 --> 03:56.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
Wikipdia Foundation's digital security trainings and most

03:56.000 --> 04:00.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
importantly engage on your terms. Ask questions, learn about

04:00.000 --> 04:04.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
projects, and raise concerns proactively with

04:04.000 --> 04:08.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
researchers. Every successful project requires

04:08.000 --> 04:12.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
research to evolve and improve over time. Researchers are

04:12.000 --> 04:16.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
motivated to contribute their expertise to help Wikipedia

04:16.000 --> 04:20.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
evolve. We need to support them in doing successful research on the

04:20.000 --> 04:24.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
projects, including balancing the need for transparency with

04:24.000 --> 04:28.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
privacy and anonymity expectations of Wikipedia

04:28.000 --> 04:32.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
editors. Help us help the researchers and other editors

04:32.000 --> 04:36.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
by reading the white paper and providing your feedback at the meta page through

04:36.000 --> 04:40.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
August 31st. Thanks for your

04:40.000 --> 04:44.000
(Eli Asikin-Garmager)
attention.
